WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.270
Hello and welcome to this
course in database engineering.

2
00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.790
Almost everyone has
used the database.

3
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.890
More likely,
information about us is

4
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.830
probably present in many
databases all over the world.

5
00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.290
But who understands
what a database is,

6
00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.090
and how important
database engineering

7
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.320
is to global industry,

8
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.600
governments, and organizations?

9
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.780
A very straightforward
description of a database is that

10
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.990
it is a form of electronic
storage in which data is held.

11
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.420
Of course, that explanation
does not even come

12
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.405
close to explaining the impact
of database technology.

13
00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:33.690
To give an idea of databases
in a real-world context,

14
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.440
let's briefly describe
some typical use cases.

15
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.660
For example, your bank uses

16
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.805
a database to store
data for customers,

17
00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:42.965
bank accounts, and transactions.

18
00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:45.035
A hospital stores patient data,

19
00:00:45.035 --> 00:00:47.765
staff data, laboratory
data, and more.

20
00:00:47.765 --> 00:00:50.525
An online store retains
your profile information

21
00:00:50.525 --> 00:00:51.860
along with your shopping history

22
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.635
and accounting transactions.

23
00:00:53.635 --> 00:00:55.310
Many of these services have

24
00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.305
access to a diverse
range of data.

25
00:00:57.305 --> 00:01:00.680
They collect and store other
items such as your location,

26
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.585
how long you spend
on their platform,

27
00:01:02.585 --> 00:01:03.935
and friends you connected with,

28
00:01:03.935 --> 00:01:05.840
alongside many more facts.

29
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.730
Such online services and

30
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.250
social media platforms
generate enormous amounts of

31
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.725
data due to their
large user base

32
00:01:12.725 --> 00:01:14.630
and constant user activity.

33
00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.330
With the Internet
of things or IoT,

34
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.430
many extra devices are now
connected to the Internet.

35
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.050
These continual streams
of data have led to

36
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.089
a revolution in
database technology

37
00:01:25.089 --> 00:01:26.500
to accommodate the volume,

38
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.300
variety, and complexity of

39
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.485
what has become
known as big data.

40
00:01:30.485 --> 00:01:32.455
Whatever the source of the data,

41
00:01:32.455 --> 00:01:33.850
a database will typically

42
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.530
carry out the following actions,

43
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.410
all of which a database
engineer must be familiar with.

44
00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.900
Store the data,
form connections or

45
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.765
relationships between
segmented areas of the data,

46
00:01:43.765 --> 00:01:46.465
filter the data to
show relevant records,

47
00:01:46.465 --> 00:01:49.135
search data to return
matching records,

48
00:01:49.135 --> 00:01:51.985
and have functions to allow
the data to be updated,

49
00:01:51.985 --> 00:01:54.410
changed, and deleted
as required.

50
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.840
Don't worry if you don't fully

51
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.335
understand all these terms.

52
00:01:57.335 --> 00:01:59.260
For now, you're just receiving

53
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.900
a brief introduction
to databases and data.

54
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.590
During the course, you'll

55
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.615
explore these concepts
in more detail

56
00:02:05.615 --> 00:02:07.460
alongside the many
other tasks that

57
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.820
form the duties of a
database engineer.

58
00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.430
You'll learn about the concepts
of data and databases,

59
00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.890
how data is related
in a database,

60
00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.720
and different database
structures and

61
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.065
their uses, how to perform,

62
00:02:20.065 --> 00:02:24.245
create, read, update,
and delete operations,

63
00:02:24.245 --> 00:02:27.920
how to use SQL operators
to sort and filter data,

64
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.125
what database normalization is,

65
00:02:30.125 --> 00:02:32.425
and how to normalize a database.

66
00:02:32.425 --> 00:02:35.615
You'll get to build a
fully operational database

67
00:02:35.615 --> 00:02:37.490
and you will also
install and set up

68
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.740
software called XAMPP on
your computer to help

69
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.545
progress your local and
remote database learning.

70
00:02:42.545 --> 00:02:46.120
You're not expected to be a
database engineer just now.

71
00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.520
There are many videos in
your course that will

72
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.680
gradually guide you
toward that goal.

73
00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.649
Watch, pause, rewind,

74
00:02:53.649 --> 00:02:55.090
and rewatch the videos until

75
00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.905
you're confident in your skills.

76
00:02:56.905 --> 00:02:59.230
Then consolidate your
knowledge by consulting

77
00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.180
the course readings and put

78
00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.690
your skills into practice
during the course exercises.

79
00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.360
Along the way, you'll
encounter several

80
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.590
knowledge quizzes where you can

81
00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.165
self-check your progress,

82
00:03:10.165 --> 00:03:11.740
and you're not alone
in considering

83
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.645
a career as a database engineer,

84
00:03:13.645 --> 00:03:16.330
which is why you'll also
work with course discussion

85
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.505
prompts that enable you to
connect with your classmates.

86
00:03:19.505 --> 00:03:21.925
It's a great way to
share knowledge,

87
00:03:21.925 --> 00:03:25.070
discuss difficulties,
and make new friends.

88
00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.965
To be successful in this course,

89
00:03:26.965 --> 00:03:28.590
it is helpful to
commit to a regular

90
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.520
and disciplined
approach to learning.

91
00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.685
You need to be serious about
your study and if possible,

92
00:03:33.685 --> 00:03:35.825
map the study
schedule with dates

93
00:03:35.825 --> 00:03:38.710
and times that you can devote
to attending the course.

94
00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.375
It's an online
self-paced course,

95
00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:43.430
but it does help to think
of your study in terms of

96
00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:45.775
regular attendance in
a learning institute.

97
00:03:45.775 --> 00:03:48.110
In summary, this course provides

98
00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.255
you with a complete
introduction to databases,

99
00:03:50.255 --> 00:03:52.310
and it's part of a program
of courses that lead

100
00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.410
you toward a career in
database engineering.